# Two closed languages

There are two engines and each speaks its own closed language. **Never mix
them.**

**The Descent** — navigation, investigation, rooms — speaks **VOCAB.md**: gates
(`flags`, `items`) and deltas (`say`, `set`, `unset`, `give`, `take`, `adjust`,
`journal`, `refuse`, `goto`, `addObject`, `removeObject`, `prompt`, `fight`,
`end`).

**The Lots** — dice, combat, everything it spends — speaks **GRAMMAR.md**:
pipeline hooks (`on_roll`, `skill_window`, `on_score`, `on_hold`, `on_block`,
`on_turn_end`) and closed effect verbs (`add`, `mult`, `mana`, `burn`, `hp`,
`block`, `wild`, `promote`, `seal`, `curse`).

**`fight:` is the only doorway.** The Descent finds what the Lots spend. A
merchant or a dialogue resolves inside the Descent; only `fight:` crosses over,
and it returns.

## the closure rule

Unknown words **fail at load, never silently at play**, and the error names the
path to the offending node.

A new word — a gate, a delta, or an effect verb — is a **VOCAB or GRAMMAR
amendment card**: one word, its tests, its lint. It is never an inline special
case, never a quiet `if`, and never something a content card teaches itself.

`lies`, `counter` and `dropObols` are **reserved and parked**. Do not implement
them and do not use them.

If your card needs a word that is not in its language, you do not add it — you
**bounce** (AGENTS.md).
